  [1mScope flag first, though.[0m "Notify users when tasks they care about change" presupposes four things this repo does not
  have: tasks, users, a notion of "caring about" a task, and a change event to react to. Right now there is nothing to
  notify [3mabout[0m. So I don't think the first deliverable is a notifications system — it's the task model and change events
  underneath it, with notifications layered on. I want to pin down the ground before refining notification details.

  [1mThe fork that decides almost everything downstream:[0m is this static page the whole app (so everything is client-side,
  one browser, one person), or is it the front end of something with a real server and real accounts?

  - [1mStatic prototype[0m — notifications become in-page UI over local state (localStorage). Cheap, no infrastructure, works
    today. Rules out cross-device, email/push, and multi-user "who cares about what" until a backend exists — and that
    rewrite is not cheap.
  - [1mClient-side now, server-shaped[0m — same browser-only build, but the change-event and subscription model is designed so
    a server can slot in later. Slightly more upfront design, keeps the door open.
  - [1mReal backend[0m — accounts, persistence, server-side delivery (email/web push). Genuinely multi-user and cross-device,
    but it's a multi-week project and the notifications piece is the smallest part of it.

  My recommendation is the middle one: nothing here justifies standing up a backend yet, but "users" (plural) in your
  request implies you don't want to paint yourself into a single-browser corner. Designing the event and subscription
  shape correctly costs little now and is expensive to retrofit.
